#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084412 :tutorial/content "Let's take a look at the isosceles right triangle whose legs are both equal to $1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d1e8bd928fe666859f7101be65819af5f16fe81df3900941650d279eecd496.png)\n\nBy considering either of the $45^\\circ$ angles in this triangle, we find that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\sin 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opp.}}{\\textrm{hyp.}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\[5pt]\n\t\\cos 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{adj.}}{\\textrm{hyp.}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\[5pt]\n\t\\tan 45^\\circ &= \\dfrac{\\textrm{opp.}}{\\textrm{adj.}} = \\dfrac{1}{1} = 1. \n}\n$$\n\nIsosceles right triangles are very common, so the values above occur a lot in trigonometry. It's a good idea to remember them." :tutorial/id #uuid "43cdd368-7207-5d18-8f22-5e384ebbc118" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2457 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084413 :tutorial/content "Let's now consider the $30$-$60$-$90$ triangle whose shortest leg has a length equal to $1,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/67/679ce670484e57ba699030de4cf6d9ba52a5d6910d22b4beb2ad5d4e8ac4e0e0.png)\n\nNow, we can calculate some trigonometric ratios without a calculator. Let's start with the $30^\\circ$-angle. By the definition of sine, cosine, and tangent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opp.}}{\\text{hyp.}}=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{adj.}}{\\text{hyp.}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opp.}}{\\text{adj.}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, by considering the $60^\\circ$-angle, we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opp.}}{\\text{hyp.}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{adj.}}{\\text{hyp.}}=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opp.}}{\\text{adj.}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1}=\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d87688da-9a24-5f3e-9160-de9562217a11" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2460 :tutorial/title "Trigonometric Ratios for 30-60-90 Triangles"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084414 :tutorial/content "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $0^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n| Trigonometric ratio | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n| $\\cos \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $1$ |\n| $\\tan \\left(0^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n\nWhy is this so? If we look at the right triangle below, we can imagine making the angle $\\theta$ smaller by moving the vertex $C$ towards $A.$ \n\n![](images/9b/9bee0468c989067e12648e538788bcd8c7fb879e112000a942a1ee6f41d95c7d.png)\n\nAs the angle $\\theta$ gets closer and closer to zero, the opposite side $AC$ also approaches zero. Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the adjacent side $AB$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches zero (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 0^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAC \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AB.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, when $\\theta = 0^\\circ$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{BC}=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{BC}=1, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({0}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{AB}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d78c0611-a7ea-5bc0-830b-fb04311281da" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2461 :tutorial/title "Special Values of the Zero Degree Angle"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084415 :tutorial/content "The values of the trigonometric ratios for $90^\\circ$ angles are the following:\n\n| Trigonometric ratio | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\sin \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | $1$ |\n| $\\cos \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | $0$ |\n| $\\tan \\left(90^\\circ\\right)$ | Undefined |\n\nTo understand where the values come from, this time, we can imagine increasing the value of the angle $\\theta$ by moving the vertex $B$ towards $A.$ \n\n![](images/53/53d538ce2eca6af579b556bdac72d9fba9ba5e817de38ff73236a312b401c4cf.png)\n\nAs $B$ moves towards $A,$ the adjacent side $AB$ gets very close to zero, and the angle $\\theta$ gets very close to $90^\\circ.$ Also, the hypotenuse $BC$ and the opposite side $AC$ become nearly the same length.\n\nSo, as $\\theta$ approaches $90^\\circ$ (which we can write as $\\theta \\rightarrow 90^\\circ$) we have\n\n$$\nAB \\approx 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad BC \\approx AC.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, when $\\theta = 90^\\circ,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{BC}=1, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{BC}=0, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({90}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AC}{AB}=\\frac{AC}{0}=\\text{undefined.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that when calculating the tangent, we get a $0$ in the denominator. We know that we can't divide by zero. Therefore, the tangent of a right angle is said to be undefined." :tutorial/id #uuid "c602245b-ade3-5bc6-8703-b73dd0425cba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2462 :tutorial/title "Special Values of the 90 Degree Angle"}]}
